---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RandomNumberGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/RandomNumberGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="random-number-generator">
  <RandomNumberGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the random number generator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Set the minimum and maximum. Both ends are included, so 1 to 6 can give a 1 or a 6.</li>
      <li>Choose how many numbers you want, up to ten thousand at a time.</li>
      <li>Turn on “no repeats” for a raffle or a lottery draw. Leave it off for dice, simulations and anything where each draw must be independent.</li>
      <li>Sort the output if you need it in order, then copy it as a list or a comma-separated line, or download it as a text file.</li>
      <li>The Dice and Coin tabs use the same generator for the two most common requests.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where the randomness comes from</h2>
    <p>
      Each value comes from <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, the browser’s cryptographically secure
      pseudo-random generator. The operating system seeds it from entropy such as timing jitter, hardware
      sources and interrupt noise. Browsers use the same generator for encryption keys and session tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many web generators use <code>Math.random()</code>. It is a fast pseudo-random algorithm
      (xorshift128+ in V8) with an internal state that is not secret and is not reseeded. It is good
      enough to shuffle a carousel and unsuitable for a prize draw: a person who sees enough output can
      reconstruct the state and predict the next values.
    </p>

    <h2>The modulo bias</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious way to get a number in a range is to take a random integer and apply the remainder
      operator. This is slightly wrong. Suppose a generator gives 0–255 and you want 0–99:
    </p>
    <pre><code>value % 100

value 0–55   →  0–55 can each be produced 3 ways  (v, v+100, v+200)
value 56–99  →  56–99 can each be produced 2 ways (v, v+100)

Low results are 50% more likely than high ones.</code></pre>
    <p>
      The fix is rejection sampling: discard each value in the unbalanced tail and draw again. This tool
      computes the largest multiple of the range that fits in 2³², rejects any value at or above it, and
      then takes the remainder. The cost is an occasional extra draw, and each number in your range is
      exactly as likely as every other. Decimals use a full 53-bit mantissa built from two 32-bit words;
      most implementations use 32 bits.
    </p>

    <h2>Random numbers with or without repeats</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Repeats allowed</th>
            <th>No repeats</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Statistical model</td><td>Sampling with replacement</td><td>Sampling without replacement</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Physical analogue</td><td>Rolling a die repeatedly</td><td>Drawing tickets from a hat</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Each draw independent</td><td>Yes</td><td>No: earlier draws change the odds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Maximum count</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>The size of the range</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Use for</td><td>Dice, simulations, test data, coin flips</td><td>Lotteries, raffles, team picking, seating plans</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When the request is dense, such as 40 different numbers from 1 to 50, rejection sampling spends
      most of its time finding numbers it already has. In that case the tool uses a partial Fisher–Yates
      shuffle over the whole range, which makes the draw in a single pass at any density.
    </p>

    <h2>Common ranges</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Task</th>
            <th>Range</th>
            <th>Count</th>
            <th>Repeats</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Coin flip</td><td>1–2</td><td>1</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Standard die</td><td>1–6</td><td>1</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Percentage roll</td><td>1–100</td><td>1</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>UK Lotto</td><td>1–59</td><td>6</td><td>No repeats</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Powerball main numbers</td><td>1–69</td><td>5</td><td>No repeats</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EuroMillions main numbers</td><td>1–50</td><td>5</td><td>No repeats</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pick a winner from 480 entrants</td><td>1–480</td><td>1</td><td>Either</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Randomise a 24-person seating plan</td><td>1–24</td><td>24</td><td>No repeats</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Running a draw people will trust</h2>
    <p>
      Disputes are usually about the process around the generator. These steps help:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fix the rules before you draw. Publish the range, the number of winners and the tie-break rule in advance.</li>
      <li>Number the entrants first, in a list you can show afterwards, then draw index numbers in place of names.</li>
      <li>Record the draw. A screen recording of the range being set and the button being pressed answers most objections.</li>
      <li>Decide the redraw rule in advance: what happens if a winner is ineligible, or if the same number comes up twice.</li>
      <li>Keep the output. This page stores nothing, so download the text file at the time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Dice and coins</h2>
    <p>
      The Dice tab rolls any number of dice from d4 to d100 with an optional modifier, shows each die and
      keeps the last twenty rolls. Each die is an independent draw from the same secure generator, so it is
      fairer than a physical die. Injection-moulded plastic dice have measurable bias from the hollow pips
      and from mould draft.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Coin tab flips up to a thousand times and reports the head and tail counts and the longest run.
      In a hundred fair flips, a run of six or seven identical results is normal, and no such run would be
      suspicious. People asked to fake a random sequence almost never write a run that long, and this is
      how such fakes are detected.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
